A||N||6||Dan87GT||Tach V8 conversion,were to get capacitor?||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||01-14-2001||02:35 PM||||Hello,<BR>I am upgrading my GT to a V8 and I need to change the capacitor in the tach. I need a 0.0064 microfarad capacitor. Does anyone know if this capacitor is electrolitic or a non-polar? Radio Shack does not carry one? Any ideas were to get one????||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||01-14-2001||03:15 PM||||Dan,<P>That 0.0064 microfarad capacitor (equal to 6400 picofarad) is too small for electrolytic...it's probably just a ceramic disc.  Radio Shack has a 6800 picofarad disc capacitor listed here <A HREF="http://www.radioshack.com/product.asp?catalog%5Fname=CTLG&category%5Fname=CTLG%5F010%5F002%5F021%5F000&product%5Fid=900%2D2231" TARGET=_blank>http://www.radioshack.com/product.asp?catalog%5Fname=CTLG&category%5Fname=CTLG%5F010%5F002%5F021%5F000&product%5Fid=900%2D2231</A> <BR>This cap is only 6% larger than the 6400 pF cap you are looking for.  These caps usually have about a +/-10% tolerance.  For a filter application, that's probably close enough.  It looks like this part is not stocked in the stores, but must be ordered.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||01-14-2001||03:26 PM||||Well, I think .0064 microFarads is the same as 6400 picoFarads.  Does Radio Scrap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> carry one of those?<P>Don't think a capacitor that small would be an electrolytic - so it doesn't matter which way you hook it up.    ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tina||01-14-2001||09:14 PM||||Try this link<BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html</A> <P>Check under articles and troubleshooting<BR>then tach repair. <BR>No new capacitor needed in this conversion.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||01-14-2001||09:35 PM||||You can also cut the traces to pins 5 and 8 of the 25054339 chip (marked on top) and solder in a 50k ohm pot. You can then adjust it for any engine you want. Adjust it at the highest RPM possible. These aren't precision instruments and tend to read wrong the higher you go so the higher the adjustment, the more accurate they tend to be going down.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan87GT||01-15-2001||09:26 AM||||Isn't 0.0064 micro-farad = to 6.400 Pico-farad not 6400 pico-farad?????? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||01-15-2001||01:00 PM||||Dan87GT, I think<P>.0064 microF = 6.400 nanoF  = 6400 pF<BR>  = 6400  E-12 Farads<P>...but I'm just a dumb M.E.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Ask a sparky - they'd know fo' sho' <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
